      "math_academy_id": "e-14982",
      "problem": "Fill in the blanks so that the following reasoning is correct:\n\nThe notation $5 \\not\\mid 16$ means ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14114596/assets/0bae0a5f51c3e76e064e79e0f84fca3d994ff54012f630d7dd2bc6a3ae02aa2e.svg) It is equivalent to the statement that for $\\[6pt]$ ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14114596/assets/61d9319f2a01d9cf62fd2a1701c6ebf2925774936b3a60bc687fb1c88f12a6ff.svg)\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14114596/assets/05372931bea1e2d29016156cefebe65c770880362d078a4b3949aaadcabb6bf9.svg) we have ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14114596/assets/9c35e9a87d5d59359ff108abaed0f1a9a3c51ff08eafbc30e177ce4e8c428d76.svg)",
      "worked_solution": "The notation $a \\not\\mid b$ means $a$ does not divide $b.$ So, $5 \\not\\mid 16$ means ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14114596/assets/6ec0f63f68ab4d8442da8966e85564948087e868f6e246fdc14526ff1767b4a1.svg)\n\nAccording to the definition of integer divisibility, the fact that $a$ does not divide $b$ means that for any integer $k,$ we have that \n\n$$\nb \\neq a k.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $5 \\not\\mid 16$ is equivalent to the statement that for $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{ any}}$ $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{integer}}$ $k,$ we have $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}16 \\neq 5k}.$",
